— the schema says certificate.date_issued. The fragment gives the revision 6 date. Many extraction tasks would accept this. I'll include it.\n\nActually, let me be conservative. The fragment is a partial certificate. The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" The certificate number, scheme, project number, member state, page total are all present. Date issued is not explicitly present but revision 6 date is 31 July 2025. I'll include date_issued as 2025-07-31 with a note? No notes in JSON. I'll include it — it's a direct mapping from the revision history (revision 6 = current revision = issue date). I think that's fine.\n\nHmm, but actually being strict about \"don't fabricate\" — the safest is to omit date_issued. But the revision table clearly shows revision 6 dated 31 July 2025, and the header says revision 6. I'll include it.\n\n- issuing_authority: NMi — the fragment shows \"[Logo: NMi]\" and \"OIML Member State: The Netherlands\". No name/address/person details beyond that. I could include name \"NMi\" — actually the logo just says \"NMi\". Should I include issuing_authority with just name? The logo marker says NMi. Hmm, logos are \"[Logo: NMi]\" markers. I think including issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"} is reasonable. But is it fabrication? The logo text indicates NMi is the issuer. I'll include name: \"NMi\".\n\nActually, wait — should I? The fragment header includes \"OIML Member State: The Netherlands\" which maps to certificate.member_state. The issuing authority name isn't explicitly written in text, only the logo. I'll include issuing_authority.name = \"NMi\" as it's clearly indicated. Or omit. I'll include it — minimal risk.\n\n- revision_history: from the certificate history table:\n  - Initial, 11 January 2021, \"-\" (no changes)\n  - 1, 17 March 2022, \"Added production location.\"\n  - 2, 15 June 2023, \"Extension of line sizes and additional software revisions\"\n  - 3, 4 July 2023, \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN600\"\n  - 4, 17 May 2024, \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN200/DN250\"\n  - 5, 17 April 2025, \"Addition of the reduced bore version with ratio 400 and software version 2.0.60\"\n  - 6, 31 July 2025, \"Addition of horizontal housing, IP68 variant and software version 2.0.61\"\n\nRevision format: \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)\". For \"Initial\" — revision should be \"Initial\" or \"0\"? The example says revision string. The table says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\" as given... or map to \"0\"? The rule says e.g. \"0\" not 0 — meaning string type. I'll keep \"Initial\" as the string since that's what's in the document. Hmm, but consistency... The document literally says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\".\n\n- matrix_tables: Table 4 (indicating device characteristics) and Table 5 (software versions). These are multi-dim tables.\n\nTable 4: columns: meter_size, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval. Units: m³ for both ranges (per header \"[m³]\").\n\nNote values: \"99.999\" — this is ambiguous! In European notation, \"99.999\" with dots could mean 99999, but here \"9.999.999\" clearly uses dots as thousands separators (European style), so \"99.999\" = 99999, \"999.999\" = 999999, \"9.999.999\" = 9999999, \"99.999.999\" = 99999999. The verification scale intervals use commas as decimals: \"0,0001\" = 0.0001, \"0,001\" = 0.001, \"0,01\" = 0.01, \"0,1\" = 0.1.\n\nSo indicating ranges: DN50: 99.999 → 99999; DN65-DN125: 999.999 → 999999; DN150: 999999; DN200-DN350: 9999999 (with footnote *); DN400-DN500: 9999999; DN600: 99999999* (footnote).\n\nWait — DN150 shows \"999.999\" which is 999999 (same as DN65-125). Let me re-read:\n\n```\n<tr><td>DN50</td><td>99.999</td><td>0,0001</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125</td><td>999.999</td><td>0,001</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DN150</td><td>999.999</td><td>0,01</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DN200 till DN350</td><td>9.999.999</td><td>0,01</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DN400 till DN500</td><td>9.999.999</td><td>0,1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DN600</td><td>99.999.999*</td><td>0,1</td></tr>\n```\n\nYes:\n- DN50: 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65/DN80/DN100/DN125: 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 999999, 0.01\n- DN200–DN350: 9999999, 0.01\n- DN400–DN500: 9999999, 0.1\n- DN600: 99999999 (footnote *), 0.1\n\nDN600 has footnote marker \"*\" — the footnote text: \"This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\"\n\nSo footnotes[]: marker \"*\", text as above.\n\nThe DN200-DN350 row and DN400-DN500 row — no footnote marker on those; only DN600 has \"*\". Actually looking at the markdown: \"9.999.999\" for DN200 till DN350 and DN400 till DN500 — no asterisk. DN600: \"99.999.999*\". Correct.\n\nNow, should this be a matrix_table? Yes — it's a multi-dim table keyed by meter size. It could also feed model_level characteristics: indicating_range and verification_scale_interval per meter size. The meter sizes (DN50...DN600) are the models. But this is a fragment; part 1 presumably had the model family. Should I populate model_family from this fragment? The instruction: extract content present in THIS fragment only. The meter sizes appear here as the table's axis. I could emit matrix_tables for Table 4 and Table 5, and optionally model_level characteristics.\n\nGiven rule 4 says explicit 2-D tables should be extracted as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level. Hmm — for the R137 example. Here, the indicating device table is a config/model table. The meter sizes are already in the cert's model family presumably (from part 1). Since this is a fragment, I should focus on matrix_tables and possibly model_level.\n\nI think the cleanest approach: emit matrix_tables for Table 4 and Table 5. Additionally emit model_level characteristics for indicating_range and verification_scale_interval keyed by meter size? The axis here is \"Meter size\" — DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200...DN350, DN400...DN500, DN600. These are the meter models. But rows group multiple sizes (DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125 all share values). For model_level values, I could list each DN individually or keep grouped conditions. I'll list per grouped condition to match the table, or expand individually.\n\nHmm. Let me decide: I'll emit matrix_tables (Table 4, Table 5) and model_level entries with grouped model labels like \"DN50\", \"DN65/DN80/DN100/DN125\"... Actually for model_level, the \"model\" field would be the meter size condition. Using grouped conditions like \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\" is fine as a condition string. Alternatively config_level with axis \"meter_size\". \n\nActually, since part 1 (not shown) presumably defined the models, and this fragment's Table 4 maps attributes to meter sizes, I think matrix_tables is the primary correct representation, plus model_level entries. Let me include model_level for indicating_range and verification_scale_interval. The meter size acts as the model dimension.\n\nWait — should I include model_family? The fragment doesn't name a family or list models explicitly outside this table. Rule 4 example populates model_family from the table. I could add models for each DN size. But part 1 of the cert likely has the full model list (Q3 values etc.). Since this is a fragment, adding model_family.models with DN sizes from Table 4 is defensible but might duplicate. The instruction says \"for the content present in THIS fragment only\" — the DN sizes ARE present in this fragment. I'll include model_family.models? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and schema-conformant. The layered schema: model_family \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". The cert does cover multiple variants (DN50...DN600). The fragment evidences this. I'll include model_family.models with model_ids: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN500, DN600.\n\nWait, \"DN200 till DN350\" — which sizes in between? Typical water meter sizes: DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350. Revision 4 mentions \"DN200/DN250\" so DN250 exists. Revision 2: \"Extension of line sizes\". I know from typical Badger Meter / R49 certs the sizes are DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN500, DN600. But DN300, DN350 are inferred. To avoid fabrication, I could keep grouped model ids like \"DN200-DN350\"? That's not great either.\n\nAlternative: don't populate model_family; just use matrix_tables and model_level with grouped condition strings matching the table exactly (\"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\", \"DN200 till DN350\", etc.). That avoids fabricating intermediate sizes. I think that's the safer approach. Actually, for the \"model\" field in model_level values, the R137 example uses exact model ids. Here the table groups sizes. I'll use the grouped strings as conditions. Hmm, but then it's more like config_level... \n\nLet me reconsider: config_level is for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" where axis describes the dimension. Table 4 is attribute × meter_size. Meter size is arguably the model axis (different meter models). I'll use model_level with grouped model labels exactly as in the table:\n- \"DN50\"\n- \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\"\n- \"DN150\"\n- \"DN200 till DN350\"\n- \"DN400 till DN500\"\n- \"DN600\"\n\nHmm, those are ugly. Alternatively, emit matrix_tables only, and skip model_level since models are defined in part 1. The instruction for fragments: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\"\n\nI think a reasonable extraction: matrix_tables for both tables, footnotes for \"*\", revision_history, certificate metadata, production location (where does that go? There's no \"production location\" field in the sc