indicating device\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\"], column_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"]. Rows:\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN50\", \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.0001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\", \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN150\", \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.01}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN200 till DN350\", \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.01}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN400 till DN500\", \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.1}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN600\", \"indicating_range\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.1, footnote marker *}\n\nHmm, rows as objects — footnote markers: the schema for matrix_tables rows says \"rows[] (object per row)\" without specifying footnote structure. I could add \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"] to the DN600 row. That seems acceptable. Or add a footnotes field on the table. The schema has footnotes[] at table level (\"matrix_tables[]: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]\"). So matrix_tables entries can have footnotes[]. I'll put the footnote definition at top-level footnotes[] AND maybe reference. Top-level footnotes[]: {marker: \"*\", text: \"...\"}. And on the DN600 row add \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"]? Not strictly per schema but reasonable. I'll add it — harmless extra key? Rule says conform to schema; extra keys might be tolerated. I'll keep the row footnote marker since it preserves info. Actually, to be safe with strict schema, maybe just put footnotes in the matrix_table's footnotes[] array: [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"...\"}]. And also top-level footnotes[]. Hmm, duplicating. The schema's top-level footnotes[] is for footnote definitions. I'll put it in top-level footnotes[] and add footnote_markers to the DN600 row. I think that